- Story & Context: Myth in the fishing village of Boca Ciega holds that Orquesta Clorofila ended their final illicit solstice performance by running long copper wires from coastal mangrove roots straight into their master compressor's sidechain input. As the sun came up, exhausted dancers gathered around a massive bubbling cauldron of yuca, plantain, and salt pork, listening as the tidal pulse of the mangrove roots made the sub-bass duck and surge against the crashing surf. When bassist Tito dropped a sustained 30-hertz note, the sub-frequency rattled the soup spoons so hard that three local power transformers blew, leaving only the sound of acoustic percussion echoing across the open ocean. Lianet stood over the steaming cauldron, dishing stew into chipped ceramic bowls while singing the refrain: "The ocean drinks the sub-bass, but the stew feeds the sky."
